WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.280
A common SOC 2 mistake is treating the audit as a one-time sprint.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.620
Teams scramble, collect screenshots, chase owners, clean up policies,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.600
and hope the report works out.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.700
That approach may sometimes get through a narrow milestone,

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.800
but it does not create a sustainable control program.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:23.780
SOC 2 readiness is stronger when evidence is produced by normal operations. Unclear scope

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.360
is another pitfall.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:30.300
If teams do not know which systems, services, environments, people,

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:31.960
and controls are in scope,

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.540
they collect the wrong evidence or miss important evidence.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.800
Customers may also misunderstand what the report covers.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:43.020
Scope should be clear enough that control owners know whether their work matters to the

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:44.040
report.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.060
Lack of ownership creates failure points.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.440
A control with no owner often becomes visible only when evidence is missing.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.420
Each control should have a responsible owner, an operating frequency,

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.680
an evidence location, an exception process,

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.420
and a backup plan.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.520
If only one person knows how to operate a control, the organization has a continuity risk.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.140
Manual evidence chaos does not scale.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:13.000
If every evidence request requires a custom search through chats, screenshots,

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.540
spreadsheets, and private notes,

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.200
the program will be fragile.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:23.020
Sustainable programs build evidence into ticketing, identity systems, HR workflows,

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:27.640
vendor review tools, incident response records, monitoring processes,

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.180
backup testing, and change management.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:35.760
The best evidence is often created naturally when work is done correctly. Exceptions are

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:36.940
not the enemy.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.480
Ignoring exceptions is the problem.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.660
Access reviews may find inappropriate access.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.400
Change tickets may miss a field. A backup test may fail.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.300
A vendor review may be late.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:53.560
These should be tracked, evaluated, remediated, and used to improve the process.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:58.900
Hiding exceptions or pretending they did not happen damages trust and prevents learning.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:04.160
Sustainable SOC 2 operations continue when the auditor is not actively asking questions.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:08.380
Teams follow the process because it supports reliability, security,

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:12.040
and customer trust. Policies match actual practice.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:18.180
Controls have owners. Evidence is retained. Exceptions are visible. Remediation is

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:19.280
tracked.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.580
The report then becomes a reflection of operating discipline rather than a stressful

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:25.320
reconstruction project.
